atic upside"], "rationale": "The district's ten-point Republican lean, Harris's dominant fundraising and incumbent status, and a unanimous Republican-leaning rating consensus make him a clear favorite, while Watson's minimal resources leave no visible path even in a Democratic-friendly midterm climate.", "watch": "A sharply worsening national environment for Republicans combined with late Democratic outside spending could compress the margin, though a flip would still require an unusual shift.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Mark Harris (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Republican Rep. Mark Harris runs for a second full term in a Charlotte-area district that leans about ten points Republican. He emerged from his primary unscathed and holds an enormous financial advantage, with roughly $1.0 million raised and $518,000 on hand, while Democrat Colby Watson has raised under $40,000. All ten expert ratings place the race in the Safe, Solid, or Likely Republican column, and prediction markets put Watson's chances near three percent. No public general-election polling is available for the district.\n\nThe 2026 environment gives Democrats some reason for hope: reporting has highlighted weak Trump approval in some of North Carolina's reddest districts, and a Green Party candidate also appears on the ballot. Still, Watson's thin war chest and the district's strong partisan tilt cap the upside. Uncertainty runs somewhat wider than normal without polling, but Harris remains a solid favorite to hold the seat.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0383, "margin": -11.62}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0153, "margin": -14.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0236, "margin": -14.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.0345, "margin": -13.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.0223, "margin": -13.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.0358, "margin": -12.0}, {"date": "202